WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
Lower voltage: 2 V with 3 Ω gives 0.67 A.
The same blue electrons jog around the complete course.

00:06.000 --> 00:12.000
Raise voltage to 6 V, keeping resistance at 3 Ω: current rises to 2 A.
The livelier jogging represents more energy supplied per coulomb.

00:12.000 --> 00:18.000
Raise resistance to 12 Ω while keeping 6 V: current falls to 0.50 A.
The deeper pit, taller steps and rougher planks represent greater resistance.

00:18.000 --> 00:24.000
Lower resistance to 1 Ω at 6 V: current rises to 6 A.
No new electrons are added and none are used up. The obstacles are a cartoon analogy.
